
 metrics_setup - Post-install setup notes for rda_python_metrics.

 'logarch' runs as the common user 'gdexdata' via the rda_python_setuid
 mechanism. Follow the steps below after running pip install.

 DEPENDENCY:

   rda_python_setuid is declared as a dependency and is installed
   automatically with `pip install rda_python_metrics`.

 STEP 1 - Activate your Python environment:

   Option A - Python venv (recommended for DECS machines):
      python3 -m venv $ENVHOME       # e.g. /glade/u/home/gdexdata/gdexmsenv
      source $ENVHOME/bin/activate
      pip install rda_python_metrics

   Option B - Conda (recommended for DAV/Casper):
      conda activate pg-gdex         # e.g. /glade/work/gdexdata/conda-envs/pg-gdex
      pip install rda_python_metrics

 STEP 2 - Wire up 'logarch'. Choose one of the following:

   Full setuid install (runs as gdexdata; requires sudo access to gdexdata):

      pywrapper-install -c|--compile -n|--username gdexdata            # compile the C wrapper, once per env
      pywrapper-install -l|--link logarch                              # install logarch as setuid
      pywrapper-install -l|--link all                                  # or link every setuid_* at once

   Simple install (runs as current user; no sudo required):

      pywrapper-install -l|--link logarch -s|--simple
      pywrapper-install -l|--link all -s|--simple                      # or link every setuid_* at once

      This creates bin/logarch -> bin/setuid_logarch with no privilege change.

 STEP 3 - Update existing installation (no sudo required):

      pywrapper-install -u|--update

      Recompiles pywrapper and reinstalls all pgstart_* setuid binaries
      using the existing pgstart_* binaries already in bin/.

 STEP 4 - Verify:

   Run `logarch` with no arguments to display its user guide.
